# Shell scripts are executed by a POSIX shell, which treats a trailing CR as part of
# the token — so a `core.autocrlf=true` Windows checkout produces scripts that cannot
# run at all. Measured 2026-08-03: `scripts/deploy-wsl.sh` (the distro-side half of
# `install-local.mjs --wsl`, documented in .claude/rules as runnable by hand) failed
# with `set: pipefail: invalid option name`, `$'\r': command not found` and a syntax
# error on `case "$DST" in`, which is the CR on every line. `install.sh` — the public
# installer — carried the same defect. This repo authors on Windows and executes on
# Linux and macOS, so the pin belongs on the file type, not on the two paths that
# happened to break first.
*.sh text eol=lf

.aof/**/*.json text eol=lf
# 63/ADR-010 §4 — the `.json` pin above does NOT match `.jsonc`, so an installed
# `.aof/*.jsonc` declaration checked out CRLF against its LF-pinned `src/bundle/`
# source: git holds ONE blob for both copies, so the mismatch is invisible in a diff
# and surfaces only as a hash the manifest disagrees with, on Windows, never in CI.
# Measured at HEAD before milestone 63: `.aof/frozen-set.jsonc` reported `w/crlf attr/`.
# One pattern, not two paths — it repairs frozen-set.jsonc and covers every future
# `.aof` declaration, and FF-6302 ratchets the property over the bundle's own asset list.
.aof/**/*.jsonc text eol=lf
# milestone 41 review fast-follow: pin the insert-scaffolded work-item templates
# to LF too — a `core.autocrlf=true` Windows checkout would otherwise hand
# insert-shared.mjs's `stripBundleMarker` CRLF bytes around the leading
# `<!-- aof-generated: bundle -->` marker, and (pre-fix) that defeated the
# strip, scaffolding a validate-broken doc whose first line stayed the
# comment. Kept as a belt-and-braces pin alongside the CRLF-tolerant regex fix.
.aof/templates/**/*.md text eol=lf
# 124 accept (2026-09-08): the pin above is narrower than the tree the lock names. The lock
# records `.aof/templates/work/task/example.feature` — rendered LF by `aof work update`, so the
# hash matched on the node that rendered it — and a fresh `core.autocrlf=true` checkout handed
# FF-12405 leg 9 a CRLF copy the recorded hash could never match: red on every clean Windows
# checkout, green on the one that wrote it. Pinned tree-wide for the same reason the installed
# bundle trees below are: every file under the rendered templates is text, and a target pinned
# narrower than its source (src/bundle/**, tree-wide) is the asymmetry that manufactured this once.
.aof/templates/** text eol=lf
# Installed loop records are byte-compared with their LF-pinned bundle source.
# Pin the dogfood copy too so core.autocrlf=true cannot manufacture drift.
.aof/loops/*.md text eol=lf
.planning/**/*.md text eol=lf
test/fixtures/** text eol=lf
test/integration/**/*.feature text eol=lf

# The shipped ACD bundle (milestone 01 / story 00): pin LF so the rendered
# member content — and therefore the content-addressed manifest hashes — are
# byte-identical on every platform (the acd-bundle-manifest-hashes fitness
# function re-renders from these bytes).
src/bundle/** text eol=lf

# The INSTALLED bundle — the render targets of `aof work init` / `aof work update`
# (chore 108). The counterpart of the `src/bundle/**` pin directly above: the source is
# pinned LF, the renderer emits LF, and `planApplyActions` (src/render-plan.mjs) compares
# the on-disk bytes against BOTH the desired render's hash and the install manifest's, so
# an unpinned checkout is drift by construction. Measured at HEAD on this control node,
# with `core.autocrlf=true`: `aof work update --dry-run` reported 21 `drift-warning`
# actions over `.claude/`, `.codex/` and `.opencode/` — `git ls-files --eol` said `i/lf
# w/crlf attr/` for every one — and update REFUSES to overwrite a drifted file without
# `--force`, so the repo's own bundle could not be re-rendered at all. Every difference
# was the line endings alone (the content was current), which is the expensive shape: a
# stale-render report pointing at the wrong cause. Every index blob under the three trees
# is already LF, so the pin changes nothing that is committed — only what a Windows
# checkout writes. Pinned per tree rather than per member: a new bundle member is covered
# the day it lands, and every file in these trees is text. Tree-wide rather than
# extension-scoped BECAUSE `src/bundle/**` — the very source these render from — is pinned
# tree-wide directly above, and a target pinned narrower than its source is the asymmetry
# that manufactured this bug once already; a binary member, should one ever land here,
# takes an explicit `-text` override on its own path.
# (The 22/R5, 01/R2, 40/04, 79/02 carry-forward: a byte-identity guard must pin line
# endings. Guarded by acd-bundle-install-eol-pinned.)
.claude/** text eol=lf
.codex/** text eol=lf
.opencode/** text eol=lf

# The git-tracked mesh records (milestone 22 / story 00 partition; milestone 23 /
# story 00 presence, ADR-002 / fitness #6, the 22/R5 carry-forward; location moved to
# .aof/mesh/ at 28/verify): pin LF so a mixed-OS / autocrlf=true fleet sees BYTE-STABLE
# record files. A node writes its presence/<id>.json (and nodes/<id>.json) with LF, and
# a peer checks it out with LF regardless of core.autocrlf — so the "byte-for-byte as
# the writer wrote it" poll-for-durability assertions hold across the git seam
# (acd-mesh-eol-pinned). The mesh substrate anchors on the .aof config home, so this
# pins .aof/mesh/** explicitly (a dedicated, discoverable pin, even though line-1's
# .aof/**/*.json rule already covers the JSON records).
.aof/mesh/** text eol=lf

# The git-tracked RUN records (milestone 26 / story 00, ADR-001 / fitness #3 — the
# 23/R3 carry-forward: the EOL pin must match the REAL nested record path). Run
# records become a git-as-bus record class in milestone 26 (the sync root-set
# carries wiki/work/<item>/runs/…), so they get the same LF pin as the .mesh/**
# records. The `/**/` between runs/ and the leaf matches ZERO or more directories,
# so this ONE rule covers BOTH the flat legacy wiki/work/<item>/runs/<run-id>.json
# AND the node-partitioned .../runs/<node>/<run-id>.json; the *.json scope avoids
# marking a future binary run artifact as text. (.aof/mesh/leases/** needs NO new rule —
# the .aof/mesh/** pin above already covers it.)
**/runs/**/*.json text eol=lf

# The committed upgrade changelog (milestone 40 / story 04, ADR-006): a pure,
# DETERMINISTIC projection of WORK_ITEM_MIGRATIONS (src/work-upgrade.mjs
# renderChangelog) — the drift guard is regenerate == committed BYTE-FOR-BYTE
# (acd-changelog-generated), and renderChangelog always emits LF. Pin LF here
# too so a core.autocrlf=true Windows checkout never hands the committed file
# CRLF bytes the generator's LF output could never match (the 22/R5, 01/R2
# carry-forward: a byte-identity guard must pin line endings).
UPGRADE-CHANGELOG.md text eol=lf

# The committed loop document (milestone 79 / story 02) — the generated markdown
# projection of the loop registry under the work directory, and the same case as
# UPGRADE-CHANGELOG.md above: a pure projection whose drift guard is regenerate ==
# committed BYTE-FOR-BYTE (`acd-loop-document-current`), where the composer always
# emits LF. With no pin, a `core.autocrlf=true` Windows checkout hands the guard CRLF
# bytes no render could ever match. Measured at HEAD before this chore, on this control
# node: the gate red with `wiki/work/loops.md is STALE — a loop record changed and
# the document was not regenerated`, naming a registry that had not changed — a false
# red pointing at the wrong cause, which is the expensive kind. Pinned by PATH rather
# than by a `wiki/work/**/*.md` glob deliberately: the hand-authored record docs under
# the work directory carry no byte-identity guard, and one of them is the non-vacuity
# CONTROL in `acd-runs-eol-pinned` (`wiki/work/26_.../SPEC.md`, asserted `unspecified`),
# so a blanket pin would silently make that proof vacuous. (The 22/R5, 01/R2, 40/04
# carry-forward: a byte-identity guard must pin line endings.)
wiki/work/loops.md text eol=lf

# STATE.md merges by UNION, and only STATE.md (milestone 129 / story 03, 129/ADR-002 §5).
# The loop merges N lane branches home serially in the primary, and two lanes appending to
# one milestone's STATE.md (`## Notes`, `## Feedback (for retro)`) at the same base is the
# COMMON case of a wave — a textual conflict on an append-only narrative would halt every
# wave. git's built-in union driver keeps both sides of a conflicting hunk in ours-then-
# theirs order, and STATE.md's frontmatter is `doc: state` alone, so there is no `updated:`
# line for a union to duplicate. Nothing else is union-merged, deliberately: TECH_DEBT.md
# carries numbered `## N.` entries a union would duplicate, VERIFICATION.md is a
# single-writer register, loops.md is a byte-compared projection and a task .feature is a
# contract — every one of those still conflicts and halts (lane-merge-conflict), and is
# rare. This line names neither `text` nor `eol`: the `unspecified` control file in
# acd-runs-eol-pinned (a SPEC.md) stays unspecified for both, and STATE.md itself stays
# unspecified for both too. Matched by git's own matcher (`git check-attr merge`), never a
# grep of this file.
wiki/work/**/STATE.md merge=union
